Why the AGPL rather than the GPL
--------------------------------

The AGPL is the GPL plus section 13, Remote Network Interaction. That clause is
the whole reason it was chosen, and it is worth being precise about what it does
and does not do.

This is a *federation* protocol. The plausible way for the ecosystem to be
damaged is not someone copying the code and shipping it under a different name --
the AGPL covers that -- but someone running a modified node as a hosted service,
so the federation protocol exists in practice while the code that keeps it
working stays private. Section 13 is what closes that. GPL-3.0 does not: it
covers distribution, not network use, so a private hosted fork is legal under
GPL-3.0 and illegal under the AGPL.

What the licence does NOT do
----------------------------

It does not protect the *protocol*. Anyone may implement the wire format from a
specification without owing anything, and that is deliberate. A federation
protocol is only healthy with independent implementations, so the aim here is
the normal arrangement for a networked project: the specification stays open so
anyone can write a server, and the reference implementation stays copyleft so
nobody can take it closed. If a protocol specification is published alongside
this code, it should carry its own open licence (CC BY-SA or similar) rather
than inheriting the AGPL, because a specification nobody may read is not a
specification.

Adoption cost, stated plainly
-----------------------------

The AGPL is a real constraint. Some organisations are prohibited from
deploying it, and commercial use generally requires that you offer your
modifications. That is the trade being made, and it is a deliberate one rather
than an oversight.

Licensing third-party components
--------------------------------

irc-serve has no third-party runtime dependencies. The build requires a C11
compiler and CMake. If that changes, the corresponding licences must be recorded
here.
